{# table; title: Card Resolution Engine Mechanics; date: 2026-10-14; scope: docs/; author: Rocco Ruscitti; system: C.A.R.D.S.; project: OETH #}
{# labels, oeth, cards, mechanics, resolution, combat, ttrpg, report #}

A mechanical report specifying core resolution engines for the C.A.R.D.S. (Character Action Resolution
and Decision System) tabletop framework in the OETH ruleset. This document addresses the core systemic
gaps identified in {@ link, content-report.fey; desc: the OETH Content Audit @} (Sections II.A and XIII.3),
specifically defining deterministic criticals, target number orientation, skill check resolution, and
damage mechanics using a standard 54-card deck (52 suited cards plus Red and Black Jokers).


  I. Executive Summary & Design Constraints

Traditional tabletop roleplaying games rely predominantly on independent polyhedral dice rolls (such as
the d20 system or multi-d6 pools). While familiar, dice generate flat or static probability distributions
where each roll is memoryless: rolling a natural 1 does not alter the likelihood of the next roll being a
natural 20.

C.A.R.D.S. replaces dice with physical cards to realize five fundamental gameplay pillars:

  -  *Deterministic Criticals*: In a 54-card deck, critical success and critical failure frequencies
     are mathematically guaranteed per deck cycle. A player cannot go an entire campaign arc without a
     critical triumph, nor can they suffer statistically impossible runs of endless fumbles.
  -  *Dynamic Probability & Card Counting*: Every card drawn alters the remaining composition of the
     deck. Drawing three low cards increases the probability that upcoming draws will yield high cards,
     giving players meaningful tactical foreknowledge.
  -  *Classified Multi-Dimensional Data*: A single card carries Rank (numerical magnitude), Suit (narrative
     faculty/elemental affinity), and Color (binary state). A single draw can simultaneously resolve
     accuracy, damage, hit location, and secondary status conditions without rolling multiple disparate dice.
  -  *Deterministic Critical Spectrum*: The two Jokers serve as absolute boundaries:
     -  #[ hl; fg: red; bold: true ] Red Joker #: !Critical Success! (Ultimate triumph, maximum damage,
        tactical surge, bonus action, or instant recovery).
     -  #[ hl; fg: black; bg: white; bold: true ] Black Joker #: !Critical Failure! (Catastrophic fumble,
        weapon malfunction, environmental reversal, or severe tactical complication).
  -  *Deck Reshuffle Trigger*: The moment either Joker is drawn and its action resolves, the discard pile
     is gathered, shuffled, and reset at the end of the current combat round. This preserves the 1-per-cycle
     critical guarantee while preventing degenerate deck exhaustion exploits.

This report presents three distinct, mathematically rigorous, and table-tested resolution mechanics.
Each model handles both general Skill Checks (action difficulty / saves) and Combat Damage Resolution,
balancing table speed against tactical player agency.


  II. Deck Architecture & Mathematical Baseline

A standard poker deck with two distinct Jokers contains exactly 54 cards:

| Card Category     | Count | Rank / Value                | Probability (Fresh Deck) | Mechanical Role                                  |
+===================+=======+=============================+==========================+==================================================+
| Red Joker         | 1     | Wild / Maximum              | 1 / 54 (1.85%)           | Critical Success (deterministic triumph)         |
| Black Joker       | 1     | Catastrophe / Zero          | 1 / 54 (1.85%)           | Critical Failure (deterministic fumble)          |
| Aces              | 4     | 1 or 14 (System-dependent)  | 4 / 54 (7.41%)           | Extreme values; high burst or critical edge      |
| Numbered (2-10)   | 36    | 2 through 10 face value     | 36 / 54 (66.67%)         | Predictable standard task progression            |
| Court / Face Cards| 12    | J = 11, Q = 12, K = 13     | 12 / 54 (22.22%)         | Heroic competence, tactical procs, weapon DO     |
+-------------------+-------+-----------------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+
| Total Deck        | 54    | Average Value: ~7.5 - 8.0   | 100.0%                   | 26 Red (Hearts/Diamonds), 26 Black (Spades/Clubs)|
+===================+=======+=============================+==========================+==================================================+

  II.A. Direction of Target Numbers (Meet-or-Beat Standard)

To eliminate the contradiction identified in `damage.org` (where adding to the TN made subsequent actions
counter-intuitively easier), all systems in this report adhere to the universal *Meet-or-Beat* convention:

  -  Target Numbers (TN) represent task difficulty. Higher numbers are strictly harder.
  -  Player bonuses (Skill, Attribute, Weapon Quality, High Ground) are added to the card's rank value.
  -  Penalties (Multi-action fatigue, cover, injury, darkness) either subtract from the player total or
     raise the TN.
  -  A check succeeds if: `Card Rank + Modifiers >= Target Number`.
  -  The difference `(Check Total - TN)` constitutes the *Margin of Success (MoS)*, used directly to scale
     damage, duration, or quality.


  III. Mechanic 1: The Unified Kinetic Draw (Single-Card Flow)

The Unified Kinetic Draw is engineered for ultra-fast, modern TTRPG play. Its primary design goal is
*Zero-Friction Resolution*: a single card drawn from the top of the deck resolves whether an attack hits,
how much damage is dealt, and which secondary status rider triggers.

  III.A. Skill Check Resolution

When an agent attempts an action under pressure (climbing a slick precipice, bypassing an encrypted terminal,
or dodging an incoming shockwave):

  1.  The GM assigns a Target Number (TN) based on baseline difficulty:
      -  TN 8: Routine (an experienced agent succeeds unless drawing very poorly)
      -  TN 12: Standard (professional challenge, 50% baseline before modifiers)
      -  TN 16: Formidable (demands high attribute or specialized training)
      -  TN 20: Extreme (elite specialization or supernatural aid required)
      -  TN 24+: Heroic / Planar (nigh impossible without potent magic or gear)
  2.  The player draws 1 card from the deck.
  3.  Card Ranks:
      -  Numbered Cards (2-10): Face value.
      -  Court Cards: Jack = 11, Queen = 12, King = 13.
      -  Ace: 14 (High Ace standard).
  4.  Calculate outcome: `Result = Card Rank + Attribute Modifier + Skill Bonus`.
      -  `Result >= TN`: Success.
      -  `Result < TN`: Failure.
      -  #[ hl; fg: red; bold: true ] Red Joker #: Instant Critical Success. Overrides any difficulty.
         Grants immediate standard success plus a free Tactical Momentum point (usable for an immediate
         free move, bonus card draw into hand, or defensive bonus).
      -  #[ hl; fg: black; bg: white; bold: true ] Black Joker #: Instant Critical Failure. Overrides all
         modifiers. An immediate equipment failure, structural danger, or severe setback occurs.

  III.B. Combat & Damage Resolution

In combat, weapons do not roll separate damage dice. Instead, weapons have a fixed *Damage Output (DO)*,
representing their inherent kinetic or energetic payload, which scales with the hit's precision:

| Weapon Tier | Example Armament        | Base DO | Glancing Hit Damage (MoS -1 to -2) | Critical Hit Bonus |
+=============+=========================+=========+====================================+====================+
| Light       | Combat Knife, Derringer | 3       | 1                                  | +3 + Bleed (2)     |
| Medium      | Service Pistol, Cutlass | 5       | 2                                  | +5 + Stagger       |
| Heavy       | Battle Rifle, Halberd   | 8       | 4                                  | +8 + Armor Crush   |
| Devastating | Anti-Materiel / Cannon  | 12      | 6                                  | +12 + Sever / Prone|
+-------------+-------------------------+---------+------------------------------------+--------------------+

Procedure:
  1.  Attack Check: Draw 1 card. `Attack Total = Card Rank + Combat Skill vs Target Defense Score (DS)`.
  2.  Determine Hit Quality:
      -  *Direct Hit (`Attack Total >= DS`)*:
         `Total Damage = Weapon Base DO + Margin of Success`.
      -  *Glancing Hit (`Attack Total` misses DS by 1 or 2 points)*:
         The attack grazes the target. Deal half base DO (rounded down, minimum 1). No suit rider applies.
      -  *Clean Miss (`Attack Total` misses DS by 3 or more)*:
         Zero damage dealt.
  3.  Suit Riders (The Classified Outcome): On a Direct Hit, the suit of the attack card triggers an
      inherent tactical rider:
      -  *Spades (♠ - Ruin / Penetration)*: The strike hits a vulnerability. Ignores up to 3 points of
         armor soak.
      -  *Hearts (♥ - Force / Momentum)*: The attacker gains immediate combat flow. Regain 2 points of
         Stamina, or push the target back 10 feet.
      -  *Diamonds (♦ - Precision / Flaw)*: Exploit structural weakness. If Margin of Success >= 3, the
         target suffers -2 to their next action from physical pain or system shock.
      -  *Clubs (♣ - Concussion / Shock)*: Heavy kinetic displacement. Target must pass an immediate
         Fortitude check (TN equal to Base DO) or fall Prone.
  4.  Critical Outcomes:
      -  *Red Joker Attack*: Maximum devastation! Deal `Double Base DO + 14 + Full Suit Rider of Choice`.
         Armor soak is completely ignored.
      -  *Black Joker Attack*: Weapon misfire, magazine dropped, or attacker stumbles overextended. Deal
         zero damage, and adjacent enemies gain +4 to hit the attacker until their next turn.

  III.C. Strengths & Best Use Case
  -  *Speed*: Fastest resolution possible with cards. A single draw determines hit, damage, and effects.
  -  *Best Fit*: Fast-paced tactical skirmishes, convention games, and introductory CARDS-lite play.


  IV. Mechanic 2: The Tactical Push-and-Hand Engine (Blackjack Pool)

The Tactical Push-and-Hand Engine translates classic card-table psychology—blackjack, risk calculation,
and push-your-luck—into heroic TTRPG combat. It directly implements the *Hand* and *Class Deck* features
outlined in the OETH core philosophy (`cards/_index.org`).

  IV.A. The Hand & The Class Deck Architecture

  -  *The Player Hand*: At the start of a combat encounter, each player draws a Hand of cards (3 cards at
     Rank 1, up to 5 at high levels) from their personal deck.
  -  *The Class Deck*: Highly specialized abilities allow players to seed custom cards (such as Ace of
     Spades for assassins, or King of Hearts for paladins/sentinels) into an auxiliary Class Deck (8 to 32
     cards) that can be drawn from in place of the main deck.

  IV.B. Skill & Action Resolution (The 21 Threshold)

When attempting a skill check or complex challenge:
  1.  Card Valuation (Standard Blackjack Values):
      -  Numbered Cards (2-10): Face value.
      -  Court Cards (J, Q, K): Exactly 10 points each.
      -  Aces: Variable value: 1 or 11 (player decides upon playing or drawing).
  2.  The Play / Draw Phase:
      -  The player may choose to commit 1 card from their Hand, OR flip the top card of their deck face-up.
      -  The running score is `Card Value + Attribute Modifier`.
  3.  The Push Phase ("Hit or Stand"):
      -  If the running score does not beat the GM's TN, or if the player wants a higher degree of success,
         they may call **"Push"** and draw an additional card from the deck to add to the sum.
      -  A player may push up to twice (maximum 3 cards in their active action pool).
  4.  The Bust Rule:
      -  If the raw card values in the pool (ignoring attribute modifiers) exceed **21**, the action **Busts**!
      -  A Bust is an automatic failure, regardless of how large the character's static modifiers are.
  5.  Natural 21 & Combinations:
      -  If the cards total exactly 21 (e.g., Ace + King, or 7 + 8 + 6), the action is a *Perfect Mastery Hit*
         (treated as an automatic success with a +6 bonus margin).
  6.  Joker Triggers:
      -  #[ hl; fg: red; bold: true ] Red Joker #: *The Royal Flush Joker*. If drawn or played during any
         stage of the check, the pool immediately counts as a 21 without busting. The check is a Critical
         Success, and the player immediately draws 2 cards from their deck into their active Hand.
      -  #[ hl; fg: black; bg: white; bold: true ] Black Joker #: *The Sudden Death Joker*. If flipped at
         any point during a push, the pool collapses immediately into a Catastrophic Bust. The player loses
         their remaining actions for the round and discards 1 card from their hand.

  IV.C. Damage Resolution (The Combative Pot)

In the Push-and-Hand engine, damage is determined by the total card value committed to the pool versus
the target's defensive soak:

  1.  Successful Strike: When the attack pool successfully meets or beats the target's Defense Score without
      busting:
      `Raw Damage = Weapon Base DO + [Sum of all Pool Cards / 2]`.
  2.  Poker Set Bonuses (Classified Multi-Card Synergies):
      Because the player has multiple cards in their action pool, poker combinations generate devastating
      mechanical combos:
      -  *Pair (Two cards of matching rank, e.g., two 8s or two Jacks)*:
         Heavy Crushing Blow. Add +5 bonus damage and ignore 2 points of target armor.
      -  *Three of a Kind (Three identical ranks)*:
         Devastating Executioner Strike. Double the total weapon DO.
      -  *Flush (All cards in the push pool share the same suit)*:
         Elemental Surge. Triggers the suit's special maneuver without spending stamina:
         -  Spades Flush: Cleave into an adjacent secondary target for half damage.
         -  Hearts Flush: Attacker heals stamina equal to the weapon's Base DO.
         -  Diamonds Flush: Target's shield or defensive posture is shattered (DS reduced by 3).
         -  Clubs Flush: Target is stunned for 1 turn (can take only movement, no attacks).
  3.  Armor Interaction:
      Target Armor Score acts as a flat Damage Reduction (DR) subtracted from the final damage sum.

  IV.D. Strengths & Best Use Case
  -  *Agency & Tension*: Gives the player absolute control over risk. The tension of standing on 17 or
     pushing for 21 creates genuine table excitement.
  -  *Best Fit*: Boss battles, gritty infiltration scenes, and characters built around tactical luck,
     gambling, or resource control.


  V. Mechanic 3: The Trump Faculty & Trauma Deck (Narrative Clashes)

The Trump Faculty & Trauma Deck engine is built directly upon OETH's metaphysical cosmology: the
four elemental faculties and the struggle between cosmic alignment and discordant ruin. It features
opposed card clashes between attacker and defender, coupled with a dedicated *Trauma Severity Table*
that renders wounds visceral, memorable, and tactically impactful.

  V.A. The Four Faculty Suits

Every attribute, skill, and environmental challenge in OETH aligns with one of the four suits:

| Suit       | Faculty      | Attributes & Themes                             | Opposed Antithesis Suit    |
+============+==============+=================================================+============================+
| Spades (♠) | Might / Ruin | Physical Might, Melee, Heavy Armor, Demolition  | Clubs (♣ - Subtlety)       |
| Hearts (♥) | Soul / Flame | Willpower, Charisma, Life Magic, Presence       | Diamonds (♦ - Cold Logic)  |
| Diamonds (♦)| Mind / Form | Logic, Arcana, Investigation, Engineering       | Hearts (♥ - Emotion)       |
| Clubs (♣)  | Grace / Flow | Agility, Stealth, Ranged Marksmanship, Reflex   | Spades (♠ - Raw Mass)      |
+------------+--------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------+

  V.B. Skill & Opposed Action Clashes

Actions in this system are resolved as active contests:
  -  *Unopposed Static Task*: Player draws 1 card vs GM-set TN (Ace = 1, 2-10 = face, J=11, Q=12, K=13,
     Ace High optional = 14).
  -  *Opposed Clash (Combat / Social / Stealth)*:
     -  Attacker draws 1 card + Attack Skill Modifier.
     -  Defender draws 1 card + Defense Skill Modifier.
  -  *Faculty Resonance (The Trump Suit)*:
     -  Each challenge or spell lists its governing Faculty. For example, a heavy warhammer strike is
        governed by Spades (Might).
     -  If the card drawn matches the task's governing suit, the card is in **Resonance**:
        Add a flat +4 Resonance Bonus to the check total!
     -  If the drawn card belongs to the *Antithesis Suit*, it suffers **Discord**:
        Deduct 2 points from the total.
  -  *Joker Resolutions*:
     -  #[ hl; fg: red; bold: true ] Red Joker #: *The Prismatic Apex*. Acts as all four suits simultaneously
        (guaranteed Resonance bonus) and automatically counts as Rank 15. Guaranteed Critical Success.
        Generates 2 Destiny Points for the party pool.
     -  #[ hl; fg: black; bg: white; bold: true ] Black Joker #: *Cosmic Void*. Value is absolute zero.
        Guaranteed Critical Failure. Triggers immediate environmental backlash or catastrophic recoil.

  V.C. Damage & The Trauma Matrix

Rather than inflating hit point pools where characters absorb dozens of sword wounds like sponges, this
mechanic treats damage as kinetic trauma that impairs physiological systems:

  1.  Margin of Victory:
      `Clash Margin = Attacker Final Total - Defender Final Total`.
      -  `Margin <= 0`: Miss, parried cleanly, or absorbed harmlessly by armor.
      -  `Margin 1 to 4`: *Graze*. Target loses Stamina equal to Weapon Base DO. No permanent harm.
      -  `Margin 5 to 8`: *Solid Wound*. Target takes Full Damage `(Base DO + Margin)`. Armor absorbs its
         flat rating; any leftover damage penetrates directly to Health.
      -  `Margin 9+ (or Red Joker)`: *Grievous Trauma*. Target takes Full Damage AND the attacker draws 1
         card from the deck to consult the **Trauma Severity Matrix**:

| Drawn Card Suit | Trauma Category     | Mechanical Debuff & Ongoing Complication                            |
+=================+=====================+======================================================================+
| Spades (♠)      | Structural Fracture | Broken bone or crushed chassis. Target's Movement speed halved;      |
|                 |                     | physical rolls suffer -3 penalty until set by field surgeon.         |
| Hearts (♥)      | Arterial Rupture    | Severe internal or external bleeding. Target suffers 3 ongoing       |
|                 |                     | damage at the start of each round until stabilized (TN 12 Medicine). |
| Diamonds (♦)    | Systemic Concussion | Head trauma or computational core dislodged. Target cannot cast      |
|                 |                     | spells or activate talents for 1 round; dazed state.                 |
| Clubs (♣)       | Severe Tendon Slice | Hamstrung or disarmed. Dropped weapon flies 15 feet; target falls    |
|                 |                     | prone and cannot take reaction actions.                              |
+-----------------+---------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Trauma Rank Modifier:
  -  Trauma Card Rank 2-6: Mild complication (resolves in 1 round or with quick rest).
  -  Trauma Card Rank 7-10: Severe trauma (requires surgical intervention; lasting combat impairment).
  -  Trauma Card Court (J, Q, K): Permanent scarring or equipment destroyed.
  -  *Red Joker on Trauma Draw*: Instant Decapitation / Heartstrike! Non-boss target is slain or knocked
     unconscious immediately; boss suffers double maximum trauma.
  -  *Black Joker on Trauma Draw*: Miracle of Fate! The strike would have been lethal, but struck a pocket
     watch, holy talisman, or thick armor buckle. Target takes 1 damage and zero trauma debuffs.

  V.D. Strengths & Best Use Case
  -  *Narrative Weight*: Makes every wound narratively vivid and tactically consequential.
  -  *Best Fit*: Survival horror, gritty military campaigns, dark fantasy, and high-stakes dueling.


  VI. Comparative Analysis & Benchmarks

The following matrix contrasts all three mechanics across key design and table dimensions:

| Dimension                | Mechanic 1: Unified Kinetic Draw  | Mechanic 2: Tactical Push Pool    | Mechanic 3: Trump & Trauma Deck  |
+==========================+===================================+===================================+==================================+
| Draws per Resolution     | Exactly 1 draw (super fast)       | 1 to 3 draws (player choice)      | 2 draws (Attacker vs Defender)   |
| Average Resolution Time  | ~3 - 5 seconds                    | ~12 - 20 seconds                  | ~8 - 15 seconds                  |
| Math Complexity          | Low (Rank + Mod vs TN)            | Medium (Addition up to 21)        | Low to Medium (Margin of Clash)  |
| Tactical Player Agency   | Low to Moderate                   | Extremely High (Push vs Stand)    | Moderate to High (Suit strategy) |
| Hand Management Overhead | Minimal                           | Active (Manages 3-5 card hand)    | Moderate (Hand used for trumps)  |
| Critical Guarantee       | Exact (1 Crit, 1 Fumble per deck) | Exact (Jokers in deck/hand)       | Exact (Jokers dictate climax)    |
| Lethality Profile        | Balanced / Heroic                 | Moderate / Variable               | High / Gritty & Visceral         |
| Rules Overhead           | Very Low                          | Moderate                          | Moderate                         |
+--------------------------+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+----------------------------------+


  VII. D20 Conversion & Adaptation Guide

Because existing adventures and creature stat blocks across the OETH repository (and third-party OGL/d20
material) utilize standard d20 target difficulties and polyhedral damage expressions, this conversion
table translates legacy metrics into C.A.R.D.S. parameters:

  VII.A. Difficulty Class (DC) to Target Number (TN)

| Legacy d20 DC | Difficulty Descriptor | C.A.R.D.S. Target Number (TN) | Card Rank Needed (w/ +2 Mod) |
+===============+=======================+================================+==============================+
| DC 10         | Easy / Routine        | TN 8                           | 6 or higher (70% chance)     |
| DC 12         | Moderate              | TN 10                          | 8 or higher (54% chance)     |
| DC 15         | Challenging           | TN 13                          | Jack or higher (35% chance)  |
| DC 18         | Hard                  | TN 16                          | King or higher (15% chance)  |
| DC 20         | Very Hard             | TN 18                          | Ace or higher (w/ bonuses)   |
| DC 25+        | Epic / Legendary      | TN 22+                         | Requires high spec + Red Jok |
+---------------+-----------------------+--------------------------------+------------------------------+

  VII.B. Polyhedral Damage Dice to Weapon Damage Output (DO)

In legacy systems, damage dice roll wildly from 1 to the maximum die size. In C.A.R.D.S., weapons have a
reliable Base DO equal to the expected average roll, with card rank margins providing the swing:

| Legacy Damage Die | Average Roll | C.A.R.D.S. Base DO | Weapon Category Archetype                    |
+===================+==============+====================+==============================================+
| 1d4               | 2.5          | 2                  | Pocket Dagger, Unarmed Strike, Dart          |
| 1d6               | 3.5          | 3                  | Shortsword, Revolver, Light Crossbow         |
| 1d8               | 4.5          | 4                  | Rapier, Longsword, Heavy Service Pistol      |
| 1d10              | 5.5          | 5                  | Halberd, Repeating Carbine, Heavy Flail      |
| 1d12              | 6.5          | 6                  | Greataxe, Battle Lance, Trench Shotgun       |
| 2d6               | 7.0          | 7                  | Greatsword, Double-Barreled Elephant Gun     |
| 2d12 / 3d8        | 13.0         | 13                 | Heavy Vehicle Cannon, Planar Disintegration  |
+-------------------+--------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------+


  VIII. Concrete Play Examples

  VIII.A. Example 1: Infiltration Skill Check (Picking an Encryption Shackle)

Situation:
Agent Vesper is attempting to bypass an imperial security shackle before patrol droids round the corridor.
The GM sets the difficulty at **TN 12** (Standard Technical Challenge). Vesper has an Agility modifier of
`+2` and Lockpicking proficiency of `+1` (Total modifier: `+3`).

  -  *Under Mechanic 1 (Unified Kinetic Draw)*:
     -  Vesper draws the top card: **7 of Spades**.
     -  Check Result: `7 + 3 = 10`.
     -  Outcome: Misses TN 12 by 2 points.
     -  Resolution: Because the check missed by 2 points, it resolves as a *Partial Success with Cost*
        (Glancing skill check): The shackle pops open, but the mechanical latch produces an audible snap,
        alerting the guard post down the hall.
  -  *Under Mechanic 2 (Tactical Push Pool)*:
     -  Vesper flips her first card from the deck: **5 of Hearts**.
     -  Current score: `5 + 3 = 8`. Not enough to beat TN 12.
     -  Vesper decides to **Push**: she draws a second card from the deck: **Queen of Diamonds** (Value 10).
     -  Pool check: Raw card total is `5 + 10 = 15`. (Well under 21, so no bust!).
     -  Final total: `15 + 3 = 18`.
     -  Outcome: Crushing success! The lock slides open silently with seconds to spare.
  -  *Under Mechanic 3 (Trump Faculty & Trauma)*:
     -  Lockpicking falls under Clubs (Grace / Fine Dexterity).
     -  Vesper draws the **Jack of Clubs**!
     -  Because Clubs matches the task's Faculty, Vesper gains the **+4 Resonance Bonus**:
        `Jack (11) + 3 (Skill) + 4 (Resonance) = 18`.
     -  Outcome: Flawless victory. Vesper not only cracks the shackle but recovers an unspent cryptographic
        key card from the mechanism.

  VIII.B. Example 2: Combat Engagement (Broadsword Strike vs Ironbound Golem)

Situation:
Commander Thorne strikes an Ironbound Golem (Defense Score DS 14, Armor Soak 3). Thorne wields a Masterwork
Broadsword (Base DO 5, Weapon Skill `+3`).

  -  *Under Mechanic 1 (Unified Kinetic Draw)*:
     -  Thorne draws: **King of Spades** (Rank 13).
     -  Attack Total: `13 + 3 = 16`.
     -  Check: `16 >= 14` (Direct Hit, Margin of Success = +2).
     -  Raw Damage: `Base DO 5 + Margin 2 = 7 damage`.
     -  Suit Rider: The card is a **Spade** (Ruin / Penetration), which ignores 3 points of armor!
     -  The Golem's Armor Soak (3) is completely nullified by the Spades rider.
     -  Result: The Golem sustains 7 direct structural damage, and the blade shatters an internal hydraulic joint.
  -  *Under Mechanic 2 (Tactical Push Pool)*:
     -  Thorne plays an **8 of Clubs** from his hand. Total: `8 + 3 = 11`. (Needs to beat DS 14).
     -  Thorne calls a **Push** and draws: **8 of Diamonds**.
     -  Pool total: `8 + 8 = 16`. (Safe from busting!).
     -  Total Attack: `16 + 3 = 19` (Hits DS 14 with a +5 Margin).
     -  *Poker Synergy*: Thorne has drawn a **Pair of 8s**!
     -  Damage Calculation: `Base DO 5 + (16 / 2 = 8) + 5 (Pair Bonus) = 18 damage`.
     -  Golem Armor soaks 3 damage.
     -  Result: The Golem sustains a staggering 15 damage, collapsing to one knee.
  -  *Under Mechanic 3 (Trump Faculty & Trauma)*:
     -  Broadsword attack is a Spades (Might) action.
     -  Thorne draws: **Queen of Spades** (Rank 12 w/ Spades Resonance +4 + Skill 3 = 19).
     -  The Golem draws for Defense: **6 of Hearts** + Defense 2 = 8.
     -  Clash Margin: `19 - 8 = 11`.
     -  Outcome: A Margin of 11 triggers a **Grievous Trauma**!
     -  Thorne deals `Base DO 5 + Margin 11 = 16 damage` (Golem soaks 3, takes 13).
     -  Thorne draws a Trauma card for the Golem: **9 of Clubs** (Mobility Trauma).
     -  Result: The Golem's right leg actuator is cleaved through; it drops prone and its movement is halved
        for the remainder of the battle.


  IX. Recommendations for OETH & Next Steps

To resolve the remaining open questions from {@ link, content-report.fey; desc: the Content Audit @}:

  1.  *Adopt Mechanic 1 (Unified Kinetic Draw) as the Core Resolution Engine for CARDS-lite*:
      -  Its 1-draw speed makes it immediately accessible to players coming from 5e or Pathfinder.
      -  It completely eliminates the math contradictions in `damage.org` while keeping weapon DO simple.
  2.  *Reserve Mechanic 2 (Tactical Push Pool) for High-Tier Play & Tactical Classes*:
      -  Classes with strong tactical themes (such as Gunslingers, Infiltrators, and High Sorcerers)
         can unlock the "Push Engine" as a distinctive class feature, utilizing their personal Hands
         and Class Decks while standard combatants use the Unified Draw.
  3.  *Incorporate the Trauma Severity Matrix (from Mechanic 3) for Critical Hits*:
      -  Whenever a Red Joker is drawn, or when damage exceeds a target's Wound Threshold, draw 1 card
         for a visceral Trauma outcome. This provides grim, cinematic depth without bogging down everyday
         attacks.
  4.  *Update the Rulebook Files*:
      -  Rewrite `content/basic-training-manual/cards/_index.org` with the explicit 54-card deck definitions
         and the Red/Black Joker rules.
      -  Replace the commented-out legacy draft in `content/basic-training-manual/cards/damage.org` with
         the Unified Kinetic Draw rules and the D20 conversion tables established in this report.
